; fontlib.tal - the bitmap character set, shared by clock.rom and font.rom.
;
; EIGHT wide and eight down, one byte a row, bit 7 the leftmost pixel: $81 is
; #.....#, $3c is ..####.. . One bit per pixel and no sprite port anywhere - the
; sprite packs four pixels to a byte at two bits each, which has to be re-decoded
; every time something goes wrong in it, and this is a font, not a picture.
;
; The shape is in the data. A glyph is changed by changing one byte, and a row is
; read as eight binary digits: $a5 is 10100101.
;
; THE GLYPHS THEMSELVES ARE IN ANOTHER FILE
; -----------------------------------------
; The table is asm/lib/ascii.tal, generated by tools/font512.py from
; https://github.com/alexfru/512_8, and it is included by the program rather than
; from here. That is because an INCLUDE path is resolved against the file doing
; the including, and this file lives one directory down from the programs, so a
; path written here would be a guess. Two lines in the program is cheaper than
; being wrong about one.
;
; INDEXED BY CHARACTER
; -------------------
; The glyph for character code c is at @font + (c - $20) * 8. Glyph zero is the
; space, the digits are $10 to $19, A to Z are $21 to $2a, and the last of them,
; the tilde, is $5e. So a character code is one subtraction away from its own
; offset: no table of mappings and no search.
;
; @num used to live here and drew a digit by counting up from the digits' slot.
; Nothing calls it any more - clock.tal draws through @char, which steps the
; cursor as it goes, and font.tal walks whole runs itself - so it has gone rather
; than sat in the library being maintained for nobody.
;
; Drawn one @plot per lit pixel. The eight columns of a row are unrolled with
; their masks written in - $80, $40, $20 ... $01 - rather than walked round a
; table, because a mask table means computing its address and every way of
; computing an address on this machine has been wrong at least once.
;
; Each column's bit is tested BEFORE the coordinates go on the stack, so the "not
; lit" path leaves nothing behind. A version that pushed x and y first and
; branched afterwards left two bytes per dark pixel, which reads as a font that
; melts towards the right.
;
; @glyph hands back the address of the NEXT glyph rather than taking an index, so
; laying out a run of them is a walk with no arithmetic in it. There is no
; multiply anywhere in this file.
;
; Why not the seven-segment digits this replaces: a seven-segment figure cannot
; be a right-shaped digit. The 1 has no foot, the 4 is open-topped, the 7 is
; bare, and no choice of masks or offsets changes that - the shape is not in the
; data, it is in the seven pieces.
;
; Stack contracts, checked by the assembler and tools/netcheck.py:
;   @glyph  x short, y short, glyph short, colour byte   seven in, two out
;
; Zero page $a0 to $a8. Nothing else goes there: common.tal owns $10 to $26,
; clock.tal $28 to $37 and font.tal $8a upwards.

@ZP.fx    = $a0                    ; the glyph's top left
@ZP.fy    = $a2
@ZP.fp    = $a4                    ; walking the eight rows, handed back
@ZP.fc    = $a6                    ; the colour
@ZP.frow  = $a7                    ; rows left, eight
@ZP.fr    = $a8                    ; the row's eight pixels

; The ten digits start at glyph $10, which is ($30 - $20) * 8. Written as the
; constant it is rather than as @font + 128 because a symbol followed by an
; operator only parses inside a definition like this one, and because a reader
; should not have to know that a glyph is eight bytes to check it.
; Stack: x short, y short, glyph short, colour byte. Seven in, two out.
@glyph
        STA $ZP.fc
        STA2 $ZP.fp
        STA2 $ZP.fy
        STA2 $ZP.fx
        LIT $08
        STA $ZP.frow
@glyph-row
        LDA2 $ZP.fp
        LDA                     ; the row's eight pixels, and its address with it
        STA $ZP.fr

        LDZ $ZP.fr
        LIT $80
        AND
        JNZ @glyph-lit0
        JMP @glyph-col1
@glyph-lit0
        LDA2 $ZP.fx
        LDA2 $ZP.fy
        LDZ $ZP.fc
        JSR2 @plot

@glyph-col1
        LDZ $ZP.fr
        LIT $40
        AND
        JNZ @glyph-lit1
        JMP @glyph-col2
@glyph-lit1
        LDA2 $ZP.fx
LIT2 $0001
        ADD2
        LDA2 $ZP.fy
        LDZ $ZP.fc
        JSR2 @plot

@glyph-col2
        LDZ $ZP.fr
        LIT $20
        AND
        JNZ @glyph-lit2
        JMP @glyph-col3
@glyph-lit2
        LDA2 $ZP.fx
LIT2 $0002
        ADD2
        LDA2 $ZP.fy
        LDZ $ZP.fc
        JSR2 @plot

@glyph-col3
        LDZ $ZP.fr
        LIT $10
        AND
        JNZ @glyph-lit3
        JMP @glyph-col4
@glyph-lit3
        LDA2 $ZP.fx
LIT2 $0003
        ADD2
        LDA2 $ZP.fy
        LDZ $ZP.fc
        JSR2 @plot

@glyph-col4
        LDZ $ZP.fr
        LIT $08
        AND
        JNZ @glyph-lit4
        JMP @glyph-col5
@glyph-lit4
        LDA2 $ZP.fx
LIT2 $0004
        ADD2
        LDA2 $ZP.fy
        LDZ $ZP.fc
        JSR2 @plot

@glyph-col5
        LDZ $ZP.fr
        LIT $04
        AND
        JNZ @glyph-lit5
        JMP @glyph-col6
@glyph-lit5
        LDA2 $ZP.fx
LIT2 $0005
        ADD2
        LDA2 $ZP.fy
        LDZ $ZP.fc
        JSR2 @plot

@glyph-col6
        LDZ $ZP.fr
        LIT $02
        AND
        JNZ @glyph-lit6
        JMP @glyph-col7
@glyph-lit6
        LDA2 $ZP.fx
LIT2 $0006
        ADD2
        LDA2 $ZP.fy
        LDZ $ZP.fc
        JSR2 @plot

@glyph-col7
        LDZ $ZP.fr
        LIT $01
        AND
        JNZ @glyph-lit7
        JMP @glyph-row-end
@glyph-lit7
        LDA2 $ZP.fx
LIT2 $0007
        ADD2
        LDA2 $ZP.fy
        LDZ $ZP.fc
        JSR2 @plot

@glyph-col8
@glyph-row-end
        LDA2 $ZP.fp
        LIT2 $0001
        ADD2
        STA2 $ZP.fp
        LDA2 $ZP.fy
        LIT2 $0001
        ADD2
        STA2 $ZP.fy
        LDZ $ZP.frow
        LIT $01
        SUB
        DUP
        STA $ZP.frow
        JNZ @glyph-row
        LDA2 $ZP.fp
        JMP2r2

